rules_version = '2';

// SafePay Firestore security rules.
//
// Deny everything. That is not a placeholder - it is the correct rule for this
// architecture, and leaving Firestore in its default "test mode" instead would
// be the single largest hole in the app.
//
// Why deny-all is right:
//
//   - Nothing in the browser reads or writes Firestore. The web bundle loads
//     firebase/app and firebase/analytics only; every piece of data reaches the
//     UI through the SafePay API, which enforces sessions, roles, ownership and
//     rate limits that a security rule cannot express. See frontend/src/lib/api.js.
//
//   - The API uses the Admin SDK, which authenticates as a service account and
//     bypasses these rules entirely. So denying client access costs nothing.
//
//   - The Firebase web config in the client bundle is public by design. Anyone
//     can read it out of the shipped JS and point the Firestore client SDK at
//     this project. Test-mode rules (allow read, write: if true) would hand that
//     person the entire escrow ledger, every user record, and the API key
//     hashes. Deny-all is what makes the public config harmless.
//
// Deploy:  firebase deploy --only firestore:rules
// Or paste into  Firebase console -> Firestore Database -> Rules -> Publish.
//
// Written flat, with no indentation and no characters outside plain ASCII.
// Pasting indented rules into the console can convert the leading spaces into
// non-breaking spaces, and the rules parser rejects those with an unhelpful
// "token recognition error at: ' '" on every indented line. Nothing here can
// be mangled that way.

service cloud.firestore {
match /databases/{database}/documents {
match /{document=**} {
allow read, write: if false;
}
}
}
